<div class="eyebrow">The Problem</div><h2>Knowing More Is Not The Same As Capturing Value.</h2>
<p class="deck">A lot of ambitious people are under-monetized, not under-capable. They have learned valuable skills, collected tools, built unfinished projects, developed unusual experience, or gained access to technology before the market caught up. The missing layer is deciding what those assets can become.</p>
</div></section>

<section class="section"><div class="shell">
<div class="eyebrow">What We Analyze</div><h2>Your Existing Leverage Stack.</h2>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card"><small>01 // Skills</small><h3>What can you already do?</h3><p>Technical ability, creative skills, domain knowledge, sales, operations, management, research, communication, and specialized experience.</p></div>
<div class="card"><small>02 // Assets</small><h3>What do you already own?</h3><p>Projects, code, domains, content, equipment, datasets, workflows, intellectual property, audiences, and abandoned ideas.</p></div>
<div class="card"><small>03 // Access</small><h3>What can you reach?</h3><p>Communities, customers, relationships, distribution channels, platforms, industries, geographic markets, and trusted networks.</p></div>
<div class="card"><small>04 // Technology</small><h3>What can multiply you?</h3><p>AI, automation, agents, software, APIs, no-code systems, and existing tools that can compress labor or expand output.</p></div>
<div class="card"><small>05 // Economics</small><h3>What can become valuable?</h3><p>We look for willingness to pay, speed to validation, repeatability, ownership, margin, and whether the opportunity fits your actual constraints.</p></div>
<div class="card"><small>06 // Focus</small><h3>What should you ignore?</h3><p>A useful map also identifies expensive rabbit holes, low-leverage distractions, and ideas that sound exciting but do not match your position.</p></div>
</div>
